C = [
("h1", "5. The Conditional Word"),
("p", "Claim: an announced judgment or blessing on a people carries its own condition, and the LORD says so himself. Jeremiah 18:7–10 is the rule, Jeremiah 26 shows a prophet preaching under it, and Jonah 3 and 4 show it at work on a city. When Nineveh turned, the forty days did not run out, and Jonah was not a false prophet. Ezekiel 33 applies the same turning to individuals in Israel."),
("sub", "5.1 The rule the LORD states"),
("p", "The rule is given at the potter’s house, and the LORD states it in two parts. “At what instant I shall speak concerning a nation, and concerning a kingdom, to pluck up, and to pull down, and to destroy it; if that nation, against whom I have pronounced, turn from their evil, I will repent of the evil that I thought to do unto them” (Jeremiah 18:7, 8). The second part is the mirror: “At what instant I shall speak concerning a nation, and concerning a kingdom, to build and to plant it; if it do evil in my sight, that it obey not my voice, then I will repent of the good, wherewith I said I would benefit them” (Jeremiah 18:9, 10)."),
("p", "Three things stand out. The rule covers announced judgment and announced blessing alike. It is spoken of “a nation” and “a kingdom”, which is to say a people taken as a whole. And the turning is the people’s: if the nation turns from its evil, the LORD turns from the evil he thought to do; if it does evil after being promised good, he turns from the good. The word of the prophet and the conduct of the people are bound together by the LORD’s own rule."),
("p", "The rule is the key to a puzzle in the test of fulfilment. Chapter 4 held that an announced event that does not come to pass shows the LORD did not speak it (Deuteronomy 18:22). Here the LORD says that he may turn aside an announced evil when the nation turns. The two statements fit if Deuteronomy 18:22 is read with Jeremiah 18:7–10. A prophet who announces judgment on a people, and who is sent, is not shown false when the people turn and the judgment is withdrawn. The LORD attached the condition. The tests of the chapters before stay in force: the word still has to come from him and not lead from him."),
("sub", "5.2 Jeremiah preaching under the rule"),
("p", "Jeremiah 26 shows a prophet speaking as though he knew the rule. He tells the princes and the people that the LORD sent him “to prophesy against this house and against this city all the words that ye have heard” (Jeremiah 26:12). Then he adds the condition: “Therefore now amend your ways and your doings, and obey the voice of the LORD your God; and the LORD will repent him of the evil that he hath pronounced against you” (Jeremiah 26:13). The judgment is announced and the way out is named in the same breath."),
("p", "The elders cite a precedent. “Micah the Morasthite prophesied in the days of Hezekiah king of Judah… Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Zion shall be plowed like a field” (Jeremiah 26:18). Then the question: “Did Hezekiah king of Judah and all Judah put him at all to death? did he not fear the LORD, and besought the LORD, and the LORD repented him of the evil which he had pronounced against them?” (Jeremiah 26:19). The elders read Micah’s prophecy of Zion’s ruin as a conditional word. The king feared the LORD, the LORD repented of the evil, and nobody called Micah false. The elders’ argument saved Jeremiah’s life, and the text gives no sign that it was thought a mistake."),
("sub", "5.3 Jonah and the forty days"),
("p", "Jonah is the plainest case, and the most disputed. The LORD sends him a second time: “Arise, go unto Nineveh, that great city, and preach unto it the preaching that I bid thee” (Jonah 3:2). He goes, and he cries, “Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown” (Jonah 3:4). No condition is stated in the sentence. The people of Nineveh “believed God”, proclaimed a fast and put on sackcloth, “from the greatest of them even to the least of them” (Jonah 3:5). The king sets the matter in the form of a question: “Who can tell if God will turn and repent, and turn away from his fierce anger, that we perish not?” (Jonah 3:9)."),
("p", "The Ninevites read the word as conditional, without being told so. The king’s “Who can tell if God will turn” treats the sentence as a warning that the LORD might withdraw if they turned. The outcome follows: “And God saw their works, that they turned from their evil way; and God repented of the evil, that he had said that he would do unto them; and he did it not” (Jonah 3:10). Forty days passed. Nineveh was not overthrown."),
("p", "By the plain reading of Deuteronomy 18:22 alone, Jonah’s word failed. By the reading the volume holds, Deuteronomy 18:22 is read with Jeremiah 18:7–10, and the word did not fail. It was a word of the kind Jeremiah 18 describes, spoken to a nation, and the nation turned. The difference between the two readings is not a matter of Jonah’s reputation. It is whether a prophet’s unfulfilled word may be a true word of the LORD. The volume says that it may, where the LORD attached a condition and the people met it."),
("p", "Jonah himself settles whether he was surprised. He is angry, and he prays: “I pray thee, O LORD, was not this my saying, when I was yet in my country? Therefore I fled before unto Tarshish: for I knew that thou art a gracious God, and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repentest thee of the evil” (Jonah 4:2). Jonah knew that the LORD might turn from the evil. He fled to Tarshish because of it. He did not think that he had spoken falsely. He feared that he had spoken truly, and that the LORD would do what he had always done."),
("p", "The LORD’s answer ends the book. “And should not I spare Nineveh, that great city, wherein are more than sixscore thousand persons that cannot discern between their right hand and their left hand; and also much cattle?” (Jonah 4:11). The book closes on a question, and the question asks Jonah to accept what Jeremiah 18:8 says the LORD does. The volume adds, with Study 75, that what Nineveh believed is reported in Jonah 3:5 as belief in God. This chapter asks only what the book says about the forty days, and the book says the LORD spared the city when it turned."),
("sub", "5.4 Ezekiel 33: the same turning, for individuals in Israel"),
("p", "Ezekiel 33 applies the same movement to persons. The LORD has set Ezekiel as “a watchman unto the house of Israel” (Ezekiel 33:7). The people ask, “If our transgressions and our sins be upon us, and we pine away in them, how should we then live?” (Ezekiel 33:10). The LORD answers, “As I live, saith the Lord GOD, I have no pleasure in the death of the wicked; but that the wicked turn from his way and live: turn ye, turn ye from your evil ways; for why will ye die, O house of Israel?” (Ezekiel 33:11)."),
("p", "Then the statement of the two cases. “When I shall say to the righteous, that he shall surely live; if he trust to his own righteousness, and commit iniquity, all his righteousnesses shall not be remembered; but for his iniquity that he hath committed, he shall die for it” (Ezekiel 33:13). And of the wicked: “if he turn from his sin, and do that which is lawful and right… he shall surely live, he shall not die” (Ezekiel 33:14, 15). The word “surely” in the first clauses is an announced outcome, and the passage says that it can change with the man’s turning. The oracle is conditional in the same way the oracles on nations are."),
("p", "The volume reads this in Israel’s setting and nowhere else. The address is to “the house of Israel” (Ezekiel 33:10), the standard is “lawful and right”, “the statutes of life” (Ezekiel 33:15), and the life and death in view are life and death under the LORD’s government of Israel. The passage is not a statement of how a sinner is saved today, and the volume does not make it the ground of the Body’s salvation. The Body is saved by believing the gospel of the grace of God, and the program-specific requirements of Israel’s covenant life do not pass to it."),
("sub", "5.5 The limits of the rule"),
("p", "The conditional word is not a general escape from the tests. Three limits are plain. First, the rule belongs to the LORD: “At what instant I shall speak” (Jeremiah 18:7, 9). A prophet may not claim a condition the LORD did not attach, and a failed oracle does not become conditional because its author wishes it had been. Second, the rule belongs to a word spoken by a prophet who was sent. The tests of source and loyalty come first. Hananiah’s two years have no condition in them, and Jeremiah answers them by the first two tests. Third, the rule is for announced judgments and blessings on people. It is not a rule about what the LORD has sworn, which is the subject of the next chapter, and the volume never sets it against the sworn covenant promises."),
("ctbl", "The conditional word in four passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What is announced", "Condition and outcome"),
    ("Jeremiah 18:7–10", "Pluck up, pull down, destroy; or build and plant", "If the nation turns from evil, the LORD repents of the evil; if it does evil after good, he repents of the good"),
    ("Jeremiah 26:12–19", "Zion plowed like a field (Micah); this house and city", "Amend your ways; Hezekiah feared the LORD, and the LORD repented of the evil"),
    ("Jonah 3–4", "“Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be overthrown”", "Nineveh turned; God repented of the evil and did it not; Jonah knew he would"),
    ("Ezekiel 33:13–16", "The righteous shall surely live; the wicked shall surely die", "The outcome changes with the man’s trusting or turning; Israel’s setting"),
  ], [0.2, 0.38, 0.42]),
("sub", "5.6 Joel: “Who knoweth if he will return”"),
("p", "Joel gives the conditional word its plainest prayer. The LORD says through him, “Turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning” (Joel 2:12). Then the appeal: “Rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil” (Joel 2:13). The reason to turn is the LORD’s character, and the character includes that he “repenteth him of the evil.” That is the same verb the volume meets in Jeremiah 18 and Jonah 3."),
("p", "Joel then asks the question every honest hearer of a conditional word asks. “Who knoweth if he will return and repent, and leave a blessing behind him?” (Joel 2:14). The question has no promise attached. Joel does not say the LORD will relent, and the king of Nineveh had asked almost the same thing: “Who can tell if God will turn and repent?” (Jonah 3:9). In both places the turning is a real possibility and not a guarantee. The people are told to turn, and the outcome is left in the LORD’s hand."),
("p", "The volume argues from this that the conditional word does not dissolve into a promise or a threat. A hearer cannot treat an announced judgment as certain and decline to turn, and cannot treat it as empty and decline to be afraid. The word comes as a call. Nineveh’s king and Joel’s prophet both answer it with the same uncertainty and the same action. They do not wait to learn which way the LORD will go. They turn, because the LORD is “gracious and merciful, slow to anger,” and because the word they heard could still be turned."),
("p", "It also answers the charge that the prophets are inconsistent. A prophet who announces judgment and a prophet who calls for turning are not speaking against each other. The announcement is the reason to turn, and the turning is the reason the announcement may not come. Joel’s question and Jonah’s story together describe a word built to be answered."),
("sub", "5.7 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The LORD states the rule: an announced judgment or blessing on a people is turned by the people’s turning.",
    "Micah’s prophecy of Zion’s ruin was read as conditional by the elders in Jeremiah 26, and nobody called Micah false.",
    "Jonah is no false prophet: Nineveh read the word as conditional, the LORD spared the city, and Jonah says he knew he would.",
    "Deuteronomy 18:22 is read together with Jeremiah 18:7–10, and the tests of source and loyalty still come first.",
    "Ezekiel 33 applies the turning to persons in Israel and is not the ground of the Body’s salvation.",
    "Joel 2:12–14 and Jonah 3:9 show the conditional word as a call: the turning is real, the outcome is left to the LORD, and no hearer is excused from the turning.",
  ]),
]
